    "result_text": "The full suite is green (the acceptance test is the only test). The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule that rejects creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner, responding with **409 Conflict**, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection pattern (mirroring the existing telephone-uniqueness rule):\n\n1. **`EnsureUniqueOwnerEmail.java`** \u2014 a step function that runs after email normalization. Email is optional, so a null/blank email passes through; otherwise it scans existing owners and throws a checked `DuplicateEmailException` when a lower-cased match is found (existing emails are lower-cased before comparison so a differently-cased stored value still collides).\n\n2. **`DuplicateEmailException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause for escalation handling.\n\n3. **`DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `409 Conflict` with an `errors.email` body, matching the telephone handler's shape.\n\n4. **`org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DuplicateEmailException.yml`** \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureUniqueEmail` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) into the pipeline right after `normalizeEmail` and before `ensureUniqueTelephone`.\n\nThe pre-existing acceptance test `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` now passes, and the build is green.",
